questions.detailed:
The categories differ by **scope and what is real**.

| Aspect | Unit | Integration | End-to-end |
|---|---|---|---|
| Scope | Function, hook, small component | Feature: component tree plus state and mocked network | Whole system through a browser |
| Network | None or stubbed | Mocked at network boundary (MSW) | Real or staged backend |
| Speed | Milliseconds | Tens to hundreds of ms | Seconds to minutes |
| Flakiness | Very low | Low | Highest |
| Failure locality | Precise | Good | Broad |
| Typical tools | Jest or Vitest | RTL with Jest or Vitest | Playwright, Cypress |

{{diagram:scope}}

## Examples

```ts
// Unit: pure logic
import { calcTotal } from './pricing';
test('applies 10% discount to subtotal', () => {
  expect(calcTotal([{ price: 1000, qty: 2 }], 0.1)).toBe(1800);
});
```

```tsx
// Integration: feature through the UI
test('shows validation error for empty email', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<SignupForm />);
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /sign up/i }));
  expect(await screen.findByText(/email is required/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

```ts
// E2E: real browser (Playwright)
test('user can check out', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/products');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to basket' }).first().click();
  await page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Checkout' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Payment' })).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Common mistakes

- Using definitions loosely, so nobody agrees what "unit" means; agree on boundaries and mocking rules.
- Testing integration concerns only in e2e, making failures slow to diagnose.
- Mocking your own modules so heavily that the test proves nothing.

The RTL maxim: **the more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you.**

## Implementation details versus behaviour

| Implementation detail (avoid) | Behaviour (prefer) |
|---|---|
| `wrapper.state('email')` | Typed text is visible in the textbox |
| Calling `handleSubmit()` directly | Click the "Sign in" button |
| Checking a CSS class | Check role, name, text, `aria-invalid` |
| Shallow rendering children | Render the real tree |

## Worked example: login form

```tsx
// LoginForm.tsx
import { useState } from 'react';

type Props = { onSubmit: (v: { email: string; password: string }) => Promise<void> };

export function LoginForm({ onSubmit }: Props) {
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  async function handle(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const data = new FormData(e.currentTarget);
    const email = String(data.get('email') ?? '');
    const password = String(data.get('password') ?? '');
    if (!email || !password) return setError('Enter your email and password');
    try { await onSubmit({ email, password }); } catch { setError('Invalid credentials'); }
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handle} noValidate>
      <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
      <input id="email" name="email" type="email" />
      <label htmlFor="password">Password</label>
      <input id="password" name="password" type="password" />
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      <button type="submit">Sign in</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// LoginForm.test.tsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import { LoginForm } from './LoginForm';

test('submits entered credentials', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  const onSubmit = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(undefined);
  render(<LoginForm onSubmit={onSubmit} />);

  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'ada@example.com');
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/password/i), 's3cret');
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /sign in/i }));

  expect(onSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: 'ada@example.com', password: 's3cret' });
});

test('shows an alert when the server rejects the login', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<LoginForm onSubmit={jest.fn().mockRejectedValue(new Error('401'))} />);
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'ada@example.com');
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/password/i), 'wrong');
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /sign in/i }));
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toHaveTextContent('Invalid credentials');
});
```

{{diagram:rtl}}

Replace `jest.fn` with `vi.fn` if you use Vitest.

## Common mistakes

- Querying by test id first, bypassing accessibility signals.
- Asserting internals, so refactors break tests.
- Forgetting `await` on userEvent calls.

## Difference

| | `fireEvent` | `userEvent` |
|---|---|---|
| Model | One synthetic DOM event | Sequence of realistic events |
| Click | `click` only | pointer down, up, focus, click, respects `disabled` and `pointer-events` |
| Typing | Set value in one go | Per-key events, caret handling, `maxLength` |
| Async | Sync | Returns promises (v14+) |
| Realism | Low | High |

```tsx
import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';

test('typing filters the list (userEvent)', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<FilterableList items={['apple', 'banana']} />);
  await user.type(screen.getByRole('searchbox'), 'ban');
  expect(screen.getAllByRole('listitem')).toHaveLength(1);
});

test('disabled button is not clickable', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  const onClick = jest.fn();
  render(<button disabled onClick={onClick}>Pay</button>);
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay' }));
  expect(onClick).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
});

test('rare event', () => {
  render(<Dropzone />);
  fireEvent.dragEnter(screen.getByTestId('zone'));
});
```

{{diagram:ue}}

## Details

- Call `userEvent.setup()` per test, before render, and use the returned `user`.
- Keyboard shortcuts: `await user.keyboard('{Control>}a{/Control}')`; tab order: `await user.tab()`.
- With fake timers pass `advanceTimers` (for Jest: `userEvent.setup({ advanceTimers: jest.advanceTimersByTime })`); verify current docs.
- Version 13 and earlier were synchronous; check which version your project uses.

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting `await`, so assertions run before the interaction finishes.
- Using `fireEvent.change` for typing, missing input handling bugs.
- Sharing one `user` instance across tests.

## What act does

`act()` makes React flush effects and state updates before you assert. RTL's `render`, `userEvent` (via configuration) and async utilities already wrap calls in act. A warning appears when a state update occurs **outside** that window.

## Typical causes and fixes

| Cause | Fix |
|---|---|
| Fetch resolves after the test ends | `await screen.findBy...` for the final state |
| Not awaiting `userEvent` | `await user.click(...)` |
| Timer-driven update | Fake timers and wrap `jest.advanceTimersByTime` in `act` |
| Unmounted component updates state | Cancel in effect cleanup |
| Asserting loading state only, then ending | Also await the settled state |

```tsx
test('loads and shows the user name', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/me', () => HttpResponse.json({ name: 'Ada' })));
  render(<Profile />);

  expect(screen.getByText(/loading/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  expect(await screen.findByRole('heading', { name: 'Ada' })).toBeInTheDocument();
});

test('disappearance', async () => {
  render(<Toast message="Saved" />);
  await waitForElementToBeRemoved(() => screen.queryByText('Saved'), { timeout: 4000 });
});
```

{{diagram:flow}}

## waitFor rules

- Put **one assertion** in the callback and no side effects, because it retries.
- Prefer `findBy` for appearance.
- Never use arbitrary `setTimeout` sleeps.
- Adjust timeouts only with reason.

## Fake timers

```tsx
jest.useFakeTimers();
const user = userEvent.setup({ advanceTimers: jest.advanceTimersByTime });
```

Remember `jest.useRealTimers()` in cleanup. API names differ in Vitest (`vi.useFakeTimers`).

## Common mistakes

- Wrapping everything in `act` to silence warnings, hiding real timing bugs.
- Using `waitFor` with a `getBy` containing side effects such as clicks.
- Leaving pending requests after the test, which leak into the next.

Mocking `fetch` or axios directly couples tests to the client. **MSW** (API shown for v2; verify current docs) lets the real client run.

## Setup

```ts
// src/test/server.ts
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

export const handlers = [
  http.get('/api/products', ({ request }) => {
    const q = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('q') ?? '';
    const all = [{ id: 1, name: 'Blue kettle' }, { id: 2, name: 'Red mug' }];
    return HttpResponse.json(all.filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(q.toLowerCase())));
  }),
];
export const server = setupServer(...handlers);
```

```ts
// jest.setup.ts (or vitest setup)
import { server } from './server';
beforeAll(() => server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: 'error' }));
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());
afterAll(() => server.close());
```

## Test: async product search

```tsx
test('searches products and shows results', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<ProductSearch />);

  await user.type(screen.getByRole('searchbox', { name: /search products/i }), 'kettle');

  expect(await screen.findByRole('link', { name: 'Blue kettle' })).toBeInTheDocument();
  expect(screen.queryByText('Red mug')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
});

test('shows an empty state', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<ProductSearch />);
  await user.type(screen.getByRole('searchbox'), 'zzz');
  expect(await screen.findByText(/no products found/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});

test('shows an error and allows retry', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/products', () => new HttpResponse(null, { status: 500 })));
  render(<ProductSearch />);
  await userEvent.setup().type(screen.getByRole('searchbox'), 'x');
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toHaveTextContent(/something went wrong/i);
  expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /try again/i })).toBeEnabled();
});
```

{{diagram:msw}}

## Guidance

- `onUnhandledRequest: 'error'` exposes unmocked calls.
- Keep default handlers realistic and typed from your API types.
- Relative URLs in jsdom may need a base URL polyfill or absolute URLs; check your environment.
- For debounced search, use fake timers or wait for results with `findBy`.

## Common mistakes

- Asserting that `fetch` was called with exact arguments instead of UI outcomes.
- Handlers leaking between tests (forgetting `resetHandlers`).
- Different mocks in tests versus Storybook, so they drift.

Treat state coverage as a **checklist**: loading, error, empty, partial, success.

## Component under test

```tsx
export function OrderList() {
  const { data, isLoading, error, refetch } = useOrders();
  if (isLoading) return <p role="status">Loading orders…</p>;
  if (error) return (
    <div role="alert">
      <p>We could not load your orders.</p>
      <button onClick={() => refetch()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  );
  if (!data?.length) return <p>You have no orders yet.</p>;
  return <ul>{data.map(o => <li key={o.id}>{o.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

## Tests

```tsx
import { delay, http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

test('loading', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/orders', async () => { await delay('infinite'); return HttpResponse.json([]); }));
  render(<OrderList />);
  expect(await screen.findByRole('status')).toHaveTextContent(/loading/i);
});

test('empty', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/orders', () => HttpResponse.json([])));
  render(<OrderList />);
  expect(await screen.findByText(/no orders yet/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});

test('error then retry succeeds', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  let calls = 0;
  server.use(http.get('/api/orders', () =>
    ++calls === 1 ? new HttpResponse(null, { status: 500 }) : HttpResponse.json([{ id: 1, title: 'Kettle' }])));
  render(<OrderList />);
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toBeInTheDocument();
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /try again/i }));
  expect(await screen.findByText('Kettle')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

{{diagram:states}}

## Tips

| State | Assertion focus |
|---|---|
| Loading | `role="status"`; no flash of empty content |
| Empty | Helpful message and next action |
| Error | `role="alert"`, retry control, no stack traces |
| Partial or stale | Existing data still visible while refreshing |

`delay('infinite')` is an MSW v2 helper; verify in your version.

## Common mistakes

- Testing only success.
- Showing the empty message while loading.
- Asserting on spinner implementation instead of its accessible role.
- Not testing recovery.

GraphQL usually posts to **one endpoint**, so URL-based mocks do not distinguish operations. Match on **operation name** instead.

## Component and query

```tsx
import { gql, useQuery } from '@apollo/client';

export const PRODUCT_QUERY = gql`
  query ProductDetails($id: ID!) {
    product(id: $id) { id name price }
  }
`;

export function ProductDetails({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const { data, loading, error } = useQuery(PRODUCT_QUERY, { variables: { id } });
  if (loading) return <p role="status">Loading…</p>;
  if (error) return <p role="alert">Could not load product</p>;
  return <h1>{data.product.name} – £{data.product.price}</h1>;
}
```

## MSW handlers (v2 API; verify current docs)

```ts
import { graphql, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

export const handlers = [
  graphql.query('ProductDetails', ({ variables }) =>
    HttpResponse.json({ data: { product: { id: variables.id, name: 'Kettle', price: 29 } } })),
];

// per-test override: GraphQL error
server.use(
  graphql.query('ProductDetails', () =>
    HttpResponse.json({ errors: [{ message: 'Not found' }] })),
);
```

```tsx
test('shows the product name', async () => {
  renderWithApollo(<ProductDetails id="7" />);
  expect(await screen.findByRole('heading', { name: /kettle/i })).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

{{diagram:gql}}

## Points to cover

| Case | How |
|---|---|
| Variables | Assert on `variables` in the handler or via UI outcome |
| GraphQL errors | `errors` array, possibly with partial `data` |
| Network error | `HttpResponse.error()` |
| Mutations | `graphql.mutation('AddToBasket', ...)` |
| Typed mocks | Use generated types (GraphQL Code Generator) to keep mocks honest |

## Common mistakes

- Anonymous operations, which cannot be matched by name.
- Mocking data that no longer matches the schema.
- Forgetting a fresh Apollo cache per test, causing cross-test leakage.

Both are valid; the trade-off is **simplicity versus realism**.

| | MockedProvider | MSW |
|---|---|---|
| Layer mocked | Apollo link | Network |
| Setup | Per test, inline | Server once, handlers |
| Matching | Exact query and variables | Operation name, flexible logic |
| Real link, cache, errors | Partly bypassed | Exercised |
| Reuse (Storybook, dev) | Limited | Yes |
| Typical failure | "No more mocked responses" | Unhandled request |

## MockedProvider example

```tsx
import { MockedProvider, MockedResponse } from '@apollo/client/testing';
// Apollo Client 4 reorganised testing entry points; verify the import path for your version.

const mocks: MockedResponse[] = [
  {
    request: { query: PRODUCT_QUERY, variables: { id: '7' } },
    result: { data: { product: { __typename: 'Product', id: '7', name: 'Kettle', price: 29 } } },
  },
];

test('renders product', async () => {
  render(
    <MockedProvider mocks={mocks}>
      <ProductDetails id="7" />
    </MockedProvider>,
  );
  expect(await screen.findByRole('heading', { name: /kettle/i })).toBeInTheDocument();
});

test('renders error', async () => {
  const errorMock = { request: { query: PRODUCT_QUERY, variables: { id: '7' } }, error: new Error('boom') };
  render(<MockedProvider mocks={[errorMock]}><ProductDetails id="7" /></MockedProvider>);
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

{{diagram:choose}}

## Gotchas

- Include `__typename` and all selected fields, or the cache may warn or return partial data.
- Each mock is consumed once unless configured otherwise (check the `maxUsageCount` option or equivalent in your version).
- Disable or configure `addTypename` consistently between mocks and cache.
- Mutations that refetch need a mock for the refetch too.

## Recommendation

Use MockedProvider for isolated component tests and error branches. Prefer MSW for feature-level tests, flows with several operations, and shared handlers across Jest, Storybook and Playwright.

## Common mistakes

- Reusing one client or cache between tests.
- Forgetting variables so the mock does not match.
- Testing the Apollo cache implementation rather than UI outcomes.

Redux's own guidance favours testing with a **real store** and treating Redux as an implementation detail.

## Store factory and render helper

```tsx
// store.ts
import { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';
import { cartSlice } from './cartSlice';
import { api } from './api';

export const makeStore = (preloadedState?: Partial<RootState>) =>
  configureStore({
    reducer: { cart: cartSlice.reducer, [api.reducerPath]: api.reducer },
    middleware: (gdm) => gdm().concat(api.middleware),
    preloadedState,
  });
export type RootState = ReturnType<ReturnType<typeof makeStore>['getState']>;
```

```tsx
// test-utils.tsx
import { render } from '@testing-library/react';
import { Provider } from 'react-redux';

export function renderWithStore(ui: React.ReactElement, preloadedState?: Partial<RootState>) {
  const store = makeStore(preloadedState);
  return { store, ...render(<Provider store={store}>{ui}</Provider>) };
}
```

## Component test

```tsx
test('adds an item and updates the badge', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  renderWithStore(<><ProductCard id="1" name="Kettle" /><CartBadge /></>);

  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add kettle/i }));

  expect(screen.getByRole('status', { name: /basket/i })).toHaveTextContent('1');
});

test('shows persisted basket', () => {
  renderWithStore(<CartBadge />, { cart: { items: [{ id: '1', qty: 3 }] } });
  expect(screen.getByRole('status', { name: /basket/i })).toHaveTextContent('3');
});
```

## RTK Query

```tsx
test('lists products from the API', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/products', () => HttpResponse.json([{ id: 1, name: 'Kettle' }])));
  renderWithStore(<ProductList />);
  expect(await screen.findByText('Kettle')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Create a fresh store per test (the API cache lives in it); consider `api.util.resetApiState()` if you share one.

{{diagram:redux}}

## Unit-test the pure parts

```ts
expect(cartSlice.reducer(undefined, addItem({ id: '1' })).items).toHaveLength(1);
```

## Common mistakes

- Mocking `react-redux` hooks, which tests nothing real.
- A shared store between tests, causing leakage.
- Asserting on `store.getState()` instead of the UI, except as a secondary check.
- Omitting the API middleware, so queries never fire.

Hooks cannot be called outside components, so a host is needed. `renderHook` is exported from `@testing-library/react` in current versions (older code uses `@testing-library/react-hooks`, now deprecated).

## Example hook

```ts
import { useCallback, useState } from 'react';

export function useCounter(initial = 0, { min = 0, max = 10 } = {}) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initial);
  const increment = useCallback(() => setCount(c => Math.min(max, c + 1)), [max]);
  const decrement = useCallback(() => setCount(c => Math.max(min, c - 1)), [min]);
  return { count, increment, decrement };
}
```

```ts
import { renderHook, act } from '@testing-library/react';

test('increments within bounds', () => {
  const { result } = renderHook(() => useCounter(9, { max: 10 }));
  act(() => result.current.increment());
  act(() => result.current.increment());
  expect(result.current.count).toBe(10);
});

test('responds to prop changes', () => {
  const { result, rerender } = renderHook(({ initial }) => useCounter(initial), { initialProps: { initial: 1 } });
  expect(result.current.count).toBe(1);
  rerender({ initial: 5 });
  expect(result.current.count).toBe(1); // initial only applies on mount
});
```

## Hooks with context and async work

```tsx
const wrapper = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => (
  <QueryClientProvider client={new QueryClient({ defaultOptions: { queries: { retry: false } } })}>
    {children}
  </QueryClientProvider>
);

test('fetches user', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/me', () => HttpResponse.json({ name: 'Ada' })));
  const { result } = renderHook(() => useCurrentUser(), { wrapper });
  await waitFor(() => expect(result.current.data?.name).toBe('Ada'));
});
```

{{diagram:hook}}

## When to test through a component

If a hook exists only to serve one component, the component test already covers it with more realism. Test hooks directly when they are **shared, library-like or have tricky logic** (debounce, pagination, subscriptions).

## Common mistakes

- Destructuring `result.current` once, which goes stale; read it fresh.
- Forgetting `act` for state updates.
- Not creating a new QueryClient per test.
- Testing implementation (calls to `useState`) rather than returned behaviour.

## What axe can and cannot do

axe-core detects a **subset** of WCAG issues, commonly cited as a minority to around half depending on the measure. It cannot judge whether alt text is meaningful or whether focus order makes sense. Treat it as a regression net.

## Accessible checkout form

```tsx
export function CheckoutForm({ onSubmit }: { onSubmit: (v: Record<string, string>) => void }) {
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState<Record<string, string>>({});
  const postcodeRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);

  function handle(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const data = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(e.currentTarget)) as Record<string, string>;
    const next: Record<string, string> = {};
    if (!/^[A-Z0-9 ]{3,8}$/i.test(data.postcode ?? '')) next.postcode = 'Enter a valid postcode';
    setErrors(next);
    if (next.postcode) postcodeRef.current?.focus(); else onSubmit(data);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handle} noValidate aria-labelledby="checkout-title">
      <h2 id="checkout-title">Delivery details</h2>
      <label htmlFor="name">Full name</label>
      <input id="name" name="name" autoComplete="name" required />
      <label htmlFor="postcode">Postcode</label>
      <input
        id="postcode" name="postcode" ref={postcodeRef} autoComplete="postal-code"
        aria-invalid={Boolean(errors.postcode)}
        aria-describedby={errors.postcode ? 'postcode-error' : undefined}
      />
      {errors.postcode && <p id="postcode-error" role="alert">{errors.postcode}</p>}
      <button type="submit">Continue to payment</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Tests

```tsx
import { axe, toHaveNoViolations } from 'jest-axe';
expect.extend(toHaveNoViolations);

test('has no detectable axe violations', async () => {
  const { container } = render(<CheckoutForm onSubmit={jest.fn()} />);
  expect(await axe(container)).toHaveNoViolations();
});

test('announces error, links it to the field and moves focus', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  const { container } = render(<CheckoutForm onSubmit={jest.fn()} />);
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/full name/i), 'Ada');
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /continue to payment/i }));

  const field = screen.getByLabelText(/postcode/i);
  expect(screen.getByRole('alert')).toHaveTextContent(/valid postcode/i);
  expect(field).toBeInvalid();
  expect(field).toHaveAccessibleDescription(/valid postcode/i);
  expect(field).toHaveFocus();
  expect(await axe(container)).toHaveNoViolations(); // error state too
});
```

{{diagram:a11y}}

## Notes

- jsdom does not compute layout or real colours, so colour-contrast rules do not work there; test contrast in a real browser (Playwright with axe, or Lighthouse).
- `toHaveAccessibleDescription` comes from jest-dom; verify it exists in your version.
- Run axe on **each state** (error, loading, dialog open).

## Common mistakes

- Treating a green axe run as compliance.
- Testing only the initial state.
- Using placeholder instead of label.
- Silencing rules without a documented reason.

End-to-end tests find API drift late and flakily. **Contract tests** catch it early, per service pair.

## Consumer-driven flow

{{diagram:pact}}

1. **Consumer test**: frontend code runs against a Pact mock server and records the interactions it relies on.
2. **Contract**: a JSON pact file generated by the test run.
3. **Publish** to a Pact Broker (or a hosted equivalent).
4. **Provider verification**: provider CI replays each interaction against its real API with prepared state.
5. **can-i-deploy**: deployment gate that checks verified compatibility.

## Consumer example (Pact JS; API evolves, verify docs)

```ts
import { PactV3, MatchersV3 } from '@pact-foundation/pact';
const { like, eachLike } = MatchersV3;

const provider = new PactV3({ consumer: 'web-shop', provider: 'catalogue-api' });

test('fetches products', async () => {
  provider
    .given('products exist')
    .uponReceiving('a request for products')
    .withRequest({ method: 'GET', path: '/products' })
    .willRespondWith({
      status: 200,
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: eachLike({ id: like(1), name: like('Kettle'), price: like(29.5) }),
    });

  await provider.executeTest(async (mock) => {
    const products = await fetchProducts(mock.url);
    expect(products[0]).toHaveProperty('name');
  });
});
```

## Where it fits

| Question | Tool |
|---|---|
| Does my UI work with this response shape? | Integration test with MSW |
| Does the real API still honour that shape? | Contract test |
| Does the whole journey work? | E2E |

Alternatives: OpenAPI-based validation, schema checks in CI, or bi-directional contracts; GraphQL teams use schema diffing and operation checks.

## Common mistakes

- Contracting every field instead of what the consumer uses.
- Exact values where matchers (types, regex) suffice, making tests brittle.
- Skipping provider states.
- Treating Pact as functional testing of the provider.

Supertest suits Express, Fastify (via its server instance) and similar Node HTTP apps.

## Structure

```ts
// app.ts
export function buildApp(deps: { orders: OrderRepo }) {
  const app = express();
  app.use(express.json());
  app.post('/orders', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
    const parsed = OrderSchema.safeParse(req.body);
    if (!parsed.success) return res.status(422).json({ errors: parsed.error.flatten() });
    const order = await deps.orders.create(parsed.data, req.user.id);
    res.status(201).location(`/orders/${order.id}`).json(order);
  });
  return app;
}
// server.ts: buildApp(...).listen(3000)
```

```ts
import request from 'supertest';

describe('POST /orders', () => {
  const app = buildApp({ orders: new InMemoryOrderRepo() });
  const token = signTestToken({ sub: 'user-1' });

  it('creates an order', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/orders')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`)
      .send({ items: [{ sku: 'K1', qty: 2 }] })
      .expect(201)
      .expect('Content-Type', /json/);

    expect(res.body).toMatchObject({ status: 'pending' });
    expect(res.headers.location).toMatch(/^\/orders\//);
  });

  it('rejects invalid payload', async () => {
    const res = await request(app).post('/orders').set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`).send({ items: [] });
    expect(res.status).toBe(422);
  });

  it('requires authentication', async () => {
    await request(app).post('/orders').send({}).expect(401);
  });
});
```

